-- title:
--   Dilates of integer lattice points lie in the scaled lattice
-- statement:
--   Let $\Lambda_d := \mathbb{Z}^d$ denote the integer lattice inside $\mathbb{R}^d$ (Lean:
--   `lattice d`, the points of `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)` all of whose coordinates are integers), and
--   for a real number $h$ let $h\Lambda_d$ denote the scaled lattice `scaledLattice d h`, the image of
--   $\Lambda_d$ under multiplication by $h$.
--
--   Let $h \in \mathbb{R}$ and let $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be a lattice point, $x \in \mathbb{Z}^d$. Then
--
--   $$h\,x \;\in\; h\mathbb{Z}^d .$$
--
--   This is the elementary compatibility between the two lattices used throughout the passage from the
--   discrete theory on $\mathbb{Z}^d$ to the mesh-$h$ theory on $h\mathbb{Z}^d$: any statement proved
--   for integer lattice points can be transported to the scaled lattice by dilation.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis whatsoever is placed on $h$: the scalar may be zero or
--   negative. For $h = 0$ the statement degenerates to $0 \in \{0\}$, which is still true, so the
--   lemma is stated in the widest possible generality rather than under the usual standing assumption
--   $h > 0$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ThinCones.lean#L262-L269

